L000166	
L000167	We explain the FSS behaviors of $\xi_y/L$. According to the definition of $\xi_y$, an asymptotic relation reads $(\xi_y/L)^2 \sim \langle M^2_y \rangle/\langle M^2_{yk} \rangle$. Using Eqs.~(\ref{Equ_Gy}) and (\ref{Equ_f1}), the scaling formula of $(\xi_y/L)^2$ is written as~\cite{yao2024nonclassical}
L000168	\begin{equation}~\label{Equ_xi}
L000169		(\xi_y/L)^2=L^{q}(a+b L^{-\omega})+c
L000170	\end{equation}
L000171	with $c$ a background term. By fits, we obtain $q=0.533(2)$, $0.52(1)$, $0.52(1)$ and $0.505(9)$ as well as $\chi^2/{\rm DOF} \approx 0.3$, $1.7$, $0.3$ and $1.5$, with $L_{\rm min}=8$, $16$, $24$ and $16$, for ${\rm P_1}$, ${\rm P_2}$, ${\rm P_3}$ and ${\rm P_4}$, respectively. The power-law divergence of $\xi_y/L$ upon increasing $L$, shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig3}(d), is therefore related to the LR order.
L000172	
L000173	The values of $q$ extracted from different parameter sets are close to each other (Table~\ref{tab1}). Such a universality provides further evidence of Goldstone-mode effects. A final estimate of $q$ is $q=0.51(2)$, which might be exactly identical to $1/2$ and remains to be theoretically derived.
L000174	
L000175	\begin{figure}[t]
